{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And this is to you the tamei.</b> The reason for His saying ״וְזֶה״ is according to what they explained in Torat Kohanim in the preceding section, which deals with the impurity of a limb from a living animal — He said ״וְזֶה״ to equate it with what precedes it, for that too speaks of the law of impurity of a limb from a living animal, that it is the limb that imparts impurity and not the separated flesh, whether in creeping creatures or in domesticated and wild animals." "Further, it appears that the vav stated here at the beginning does not come to connect, but rather to allude to an additional impurity in the creeping creature, namely the impurity of its blood, that it imparts impurity like its flesh — which is not so with a tamei animal, as the Sages say in Torat Kohanim (here); it is as though it said ״וְעוֹד תּוֹסֶפֶת טֻמְאָה בָּזֶה״ (and there is an additional impurity in this)." "And His saying <b>לָכֶם</b> — the Sages of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim here) expounded that it comes to exclude, that it does not prepare the seeds, for specifically for impurity and not for preparation, as His saying לָכֶם means it is tamei and not a preparer. Further, <b>לָכֶם</b> may intend that they are tamei for you but not for gentiles, for gentiles do not receive impurity, like animals." "One must investigate this: how does the verse not enumerate the serpent, which is the father of impurity for all impurity of creeping creatures and their like? Perhaps the impurity of the serpent is in its soul and not in its body, and upon its death the body remains empty of all dust of the earth, for no impurity clings to its body since there is nothing at all in it. And similar to this you will find that the Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 114a) that gentiles do not impart impurity in a tent, but Israel do impart impurity in a tent, and this is for the same reason itself. And the impurity of creeping creatures is in their bodies more than in their souls, and therefore they impart impurity in their death and not in their life. From this you have learned that the serpent's impurity is greater than the impurity of creeping creatures." "<b>לְמִינֵהוּ.</b> The plain sense of ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ indicates that there are many species of lizard, and for anything that has many species, or even a single species alone, the verse says ״לְמִינֵהוּ״. However, regarding the skin and the moisture (Ḥullin 122b) a baraita is taught, and this is its language: ״הַטְּמֵאִים״ — to include their skins like their flesh; perhaps even all of them? The verse says ״אֵלֶּה״. But is not ״אֵלֶּה״ inclusive? Rav said: ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ interrupts — up to here. And it is difficult: from where does Rav know to say that ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ comes for interruption and not for its own meaning, to say that there are species in it? And it appears that Rav does not deny the plain meaning of the verse, and his intention is that since we have before us an inclusion of ״הַטְּמֵאִים״ to include their skins, and the verse stated adjacent to this inclusion a exclusion, as it writes ״אֵלֶּה״, were it not for the word ״לְמִינֵהוּ״, by what means would we divide between those upon which the inclusion falls and those which are excluded? And from His saying ״לְמִינֵהוּ״, although it comes for its own meaning, it also serves to separate between those included in the impurity of their skins, since the verse revealed that there is a division among them regarding impurity — this shows that ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ also comes to mark the division. And with this the difficulty of the Tosafot there, in the passage beginning ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ etc., is resolved: ״It is surprising, since it interrupted in the matter, why do I need אֵלֶּה?״ Up to here. And according to our approach it is not difficult. Also, according to the words of the Tanna of the Mishnah there, who holds that their skins impart impurity because one follows the manner of treading, one cannot ask on him: why do I need ״לְמִינֵהוּ״? — for in any case ״לְמִינֵהוּ״ is needed for its own meaning as mentioned."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:36.394891128Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}